WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.020
Hello Internet!

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:03.640
Welcome to Film Theory!

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:06.380
Where today, we're taking the Lego challenge,

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:10.920
nope, not the one that ruins your feet, the one that ruins your childhood.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.180
Now, even though we're talking about movies created from simple toys,

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:16.540
"You know the rules, this isn't a toy!"

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.320
Uh, it kind of is...

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.740
"No, actually it's a highly sophisticated interlocking brick system."

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:28.409
Fine, even though we're talking about movies created from highly sophisticated interlocking brick systems,

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.189
I'm still not gonna be pulling any punches.

00:00:30.189 --> 00:00:36.629
I'm bringing Lego out of the hypothetical movie magic, and into the real world ...of Batman...

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.000
Which is kind of weird, since Lego is real and Batman isn't,

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.399
so uuuuh, let me clarify,

00:00:41.399 --> 00:00:45.538
I'm bringing the fictional Lego characters who canonically exist in the real world,

00:00:45.539 --> 00:00:47.620
but it's a fictional real world,

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:52.769
out of their separate fictional reality and into the reality based fiction of Batman.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:53.740
I think that makes sense.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.819
Regardless, despite us being several levels of Inception down,

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.400
Our premise is surprisingly simple:

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.000
Who is stronger?

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.570
DC Batman or Lego Batman?

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.930
Literally since the day that I first saw The Lego Movie,

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:10.439
(Which, if you haven't seen it, it's fantastic and heartwarming)

00:01:10.439 --> 00:01:12.220
I have asked myself this question.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.320
And now finally with The Lego Movie sequel coming out,

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:19.680
it gives me the perfect chance to finally answer this question five years in the making,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:25.620
and I get it, I know that you're thinking this is a stupid question to dedicate an episode and half a decade of waiting to cover.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:28.480
Of course IRL Batman is gonna be stronger.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:33.200
He is a 200-pound-ish man versus a three gram Lego minifig.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:37.589
But what I want to know is their strength relative to their overall size.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:41.220
Lifting a 10 pound dumbbell is nothing to a regular human,

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.819
but it would be a feat of incredible strength for a tiny Lego minifigure.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:48.560
So we need to measure each of them in their respective universes.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:55.500
As our real-life Batman contender I've chosen what I think is the best version of Batman that isn't made of pressed and formed plastic,

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.749
the Nolan Batman from The Dark Knight trilogy.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.700
Don't tweet at me, or do tweet at me actually, at @MatPatGT.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:02.939
I'd love to have you as a Twitter follower.

00:02:02.939 --> 00:02:04.619
I tweet very relatable things!

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:09.029
So, we'll be going with his strength versus Lego Batman in our big showdown today.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:12.569
Now when it comes to measuring strength, there are a lot of different ways to do it,

00:02:12.569 --> 00:02:14.460
so, we're gonna look at two main factors:

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.540
1. How much strength they exert,

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.839
and 2. How much damage they can take.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:20.620
Offense and defense.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.680
So let's start things off with a bang

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.420
-or rather, kick!

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:28.020
What are the best measures of Lego Batman strength comes when he's at home,

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.360
completely unaided by his gadgets,

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.420
and with nothing but his raw strength and excellent reflexes.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.220
"I have incredible reflexes!"

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.620
Yeah, that's what I just said, Lego Batman.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:42.400
And we see those reflexes at work when he's caught off guard by Alfred, and kicks him into the piano.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.060
A piano that is completely across the room.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.280
It's safe to say that he's not holding back anything in this moment,

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.120
since he's acting purely on instinct,

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:54.080
and I'm gonna assume it'd be pretty hard for Christian Bale to match the results

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.880
of kicking someone his own size halfway across a giant mansion's lounge.

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:05.369
So it would seem that Lego Batman in this instance would definitely be stronger than IRL Batman,

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.760
but to be sure, let's run some numbers.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.660
When it comes to calculating strength, we can always rely on our good old buddy,

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:13.948
Sir Isaac Newton and his second law of motion,

00:03:13.949 --> 00:03:17.620
which tells us that Force = Mass x Acceleration,

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:20.420
or, to loyal theorists who've been watching the show for a while,

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:21.140
F=MA.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:22.693
Mass here is pretty straightforward,

00:03:22.693 --> 00:03:25.780
since Alfred is the thing that's moving, we need the mass of Alfred.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:31.480
And he has the same mass as his identical minifigure in the real world of 2.98 grams.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.200
To figure out his acceleration, we need to use the information that we see on screen.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.460
Going through the movie frame by frame,

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:40.540
Alfred is airborne for 17 frames.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:49.880
Given that the footage is 24 frames per second, that works out to a total air time of 0.7083333 seconds.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:54.960
And the distance he's traveling in that amount of time across Batman's room appears to be 28 studs,

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:56.940
A stud here not referring to Batman,

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:58.040
*Careless Whisper*

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.779
but the bump on top of a Lego brick that allows it to interlock with other bricks.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:05.290
which works out to be 22.26 centimeters in length.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:12.700
so knowing the distance and the time tells us that Alfred is flying at a velocity of 31.426 centimeters per second.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:18.180
So now that we have Alfred's travel velocity we can determine the acceleration at the moment of impact,

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:22.200
and by moment, I really do mean moment because going back to our frame-by-frame footage,

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:26.640
Batman's foot connects with Alfred's face for literally one frame.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:32.120
A mere 0.04166 seconds (or 41.66 milliseconds).

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.480
Lego Batman was not lying about his incredible reflexes

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:41.180
That means that our acceleration ends up being a whopping 754.3 Centimeters per second squared,

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.910
or heck, forget centimeters,

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:49.600
that's big enough to measure it in big boy units, that's 7.5434 Meters per second squared.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.680
So plug in all that back into our good old F=MA equation,

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:58.840
We're able to find out that Lego Batman's kick is delivering 0.02248 Newtons of force,

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:02.800
(or 0.0050537 pounds of force)

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.340
that may seem like a small number, and it is, but remember, Lego Batman is a small guy.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:12.250
Meaning that that's a pretty darn impressive kick for someone of his size and weight.

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:16.540
Which means that if we're gonna make a fair comparison between the relative strengths of these two Batmans,

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:20.280
we need to measure it in terms of their strength to weight ratio.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.440
How strong are they relative to how heavy or big they are?

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:29.740
So Lego Batman's strength to weight ratio when he makes this kick is 0.00505 pounds of force

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:36.300
divided by 0.00657 pounds of weight for a strength weight ratio of 0.769.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:42.420
That's the equivalent of a 200-pound man kicking with a 153.8 pounds of force.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:44.580
Is that actually impressive?

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:47.620
Well to find out let's compare him to Christian Bale-man.

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:51.250
Now Christian Bale's Batman has plenty of solid kicks throughout the Dark Knight trilogy,

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:55.089
But all of them are a bit hard to measure based on pure strength alone.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:58.209
There's just too many variables and jump cuts around each of these fight scenes.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:03.430
That said, this simple scene in the Dark Knight Rises of him shattering some bricks with a kick

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:06.260
actually gives us something solid to work with.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.720
So, how much strength does it take to break some bricks?

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:12.200
For that information, we turn to astronaut Ronald McNair.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:16.240
Like a lot of astronauts Ronald McNair was an MIT trained physicist.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:20.740
Unlike a lot of astronauts he was also a fifth degree black belt in karate.

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:26.180
Luckily for us, those two interests intersected and in one demonstration of his brick shattering strikes,

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:33.199
he actually had the force recorded the bricks broke when he exerted 675 pounds of force.

00:06:33.199 --> 00:06:37.788
So, using the weight that Christian Bale was when he filmed the Dark Knight trilogy of 210 pounds,

00:06:37.788 --> 00:06:43.520
We're able to determine that IRL Batman's strength to weight ratio is 3.214.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:49.320
Over 4 times stronger than Lego Batman, even when you're talking in relative terms.

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:52.800
I am very surprised by that result actually.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.220
I mean, look at the difference of those two kicks!

00:06:55.220 --> 00:07:01.080
So, just comparing the strength of their kicks, it seems like Lego Batman loses out to big boy Christian Bale.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:06.289
And he's not just losing to Batman but most normal people in this instance.

00:07:06.289 --> 00:07:11.419
The average person is able to leg press 1.8 to 2.2 times their own body weight,

00:07:11.419 --> 00:07:15.640
Meaning that Lego Batman is less than half the strength of a normal person.

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:17.778
You sure you're not skipping leg day there, Batman?

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:20.620
"Who never skips leg day? Batman!"

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:22.800
But maybe this wasn't a fair comparison.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:26.690
Maybe the Lego characters' lack of knees holds them back when they try to kick.

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:30.460
So, let's see how the two Batmen stack up against each other defensively.

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:32.560
Of all the abuse we see Lego Batman take,

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:37.040
probably the most savage hit comes in The Lego Batman Movie's opening action scene,

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:41.340
when the Joker succeeds in ramming Batman head-on with his car.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:45.040
Once again, we need to calculate force which means getting mass and acceleration.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:52.180
Mass here is easy, the Joker is driving his "Notorious Lowrider" Lego set number 70906-1,

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:55.120
now available for $39.99 wherever Lego sets are sold.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:59.680
Just in case you forgot that all the Lego movies are ultimately an attempt to sell plastic toys to kids.

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:04.900
Having access to a real-world version of the Lego vehicle means that we can easily find its mass

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:11.420
0.63 kilograms or 1.38 pounds and yes that does include the mass of the Joker who is driving inside.

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:16.320
Ah, I love doing episodes where all this information just exists because there are real-life products!

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:19.400
For acceleration, we followed a similar procedure to the kicks,

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:22.700
measuring distances and times using frame by frame calculations,

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:27.080
ultimately winding up with 4.6226 meters per second squared.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:28.960
So now that we have mass and acceleration,

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:34.460
we can calculate force out to be 2.912 Newtons or 0.654 pounds.

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:36.200
Again, that looks like a small number,

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:41.620
but this time when we adjust it for Batman's weight, things change drastically.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:48.260
The ratio of the force he's able to sustain versus his weight is 99.653!

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:55.680
That's the equivalent of a 200-pound man getting smacked with 19,930 pounds of force.

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.260
And yet here's Lego Batman just shrugging it off.

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:01.280
That Danish made plastic is built to last.

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:05.060
So, it's no surprise that a Lego superhero would have the same kind of durability.

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:09.020
Surprise surprise, plastic can take a lot more abuse than a human tissue.

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:13.560
Or if so we'd assume, but to know for sure, let's check back in with our Dark Knight.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:19.080
while Lego Batman has to contend with Joker and Sauron, and Godzilla and Voldemort,

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:22.920
Nolan's Batman suffers defeat at the hands of a much more mundane villain.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:25.260
"Who else drives you to one-up them the way that I do?"

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:25.760
"Bane!"

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:26.620
"No he doesn't!"

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:28.160
Oh, but yes he does!

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:33.540
In the Dark Knight trilogy, there's only one villain who succeeds in beating Batman in hand-to-hand combat,

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:36.400
by giving him injuries that take months to recover from,

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:38.220
and his name ain't Joker!

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:41.700
"I was wondering what would break first!"

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:44.100
"Your spirit, or your body!"

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:49.800
After all these years, that voice is still so weird to me!

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:52.240
Anyway, Batman's body does break,

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:55.440
but how much punishment is Batman's body actually taking?

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.280
You know the drill by now: F=MA

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:59.800
Analyze the footage find the change in velocity during the moment of impact,

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:03.500
multiply it by Batman's canonical weight of 95 kilograms or 210 pounds,

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:04.800
and boom, you got it! The force.

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:10.780
At the moment of impact Batman's body's deceleration is 7.46 meters per second squared,

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:15.500
multiply that by his mass and you find the impact of Bane's spine shattering attack to be

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:19.780
708 Newtons, or 159 pounds of force,

00:10:19.780 --> 00:10:24.150
which is not impressive, not impressive at all actually.

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:26.360
It's not even enough to break a back.

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:30.509
Now that might seem weird at first, that we had a miscalculation somewhere along the line

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:32.560
But that's not actually true.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:35.760
What it's really telling us is that this scene was filmed practically,

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.249
they use an actual stunt actor rather than using

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:40.700
CG to make the impact look more brutal.

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:42.480
And when you're filming stuff for real,

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:46.860
you don't want the actor to be subject to forces that would actually break their back.

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:49.979
Bane may not be pulling any punches in the fictional world of Batman,

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:55.120
But apparently Bane's actor Tom Hardy here in the real world does, probably for the best.

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:58.100
So to be a bit more fair to the live-action version of Batman,

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:01.420
we can probably compare his body to that of a typical human

00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:05.420
to get a more accurate estimate for how much damage he could take from a supervillain like Bane.

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:10.080
According to Ali Bydon, a neurosurgeon at Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine,

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:13.660
a spinal injury like the one that we see in the movie would require about

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:19.109
3,000 Newtons of force or about 674.4 pounds of force

00:11:19.109 --> 00:11:21.480
It's an impressive number to be sure,

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:27.860
but it's a far cry from LEGO Batman's weight adjusted 19,930 pounds of force,

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:29.480
So we're tied 1-1.

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:33.480
In terms of durability, small plastic figurines blow humanity out of the water,

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:38.300
but when it comes to feats of kicking strength, movable leg joints are apparently super useful.

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:40.660
Except, there's something else here.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:45.340
Something that clearly decides this Batman buff boy battle once and for all.

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:51.180
You see, punches and kicks aren't the best way to determine Lego Batman's actual strength.

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:58.080
Him at his strongest is actually showcased in something much more mundane and commonplace in the Lego-verse: building.

00:11:58.080 --> 00:11:59.380
In the original Lego movie,

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:04.300
they make a big big deal out of the fact that certain people in the LEGO universe are master builders,

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:08.420
making people like Batman, Emmet and Unikitty part of this elite group.

00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:12.420
"Master builders spend years training themselves to clear their minds enough,

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:19.940
and yet, your mind is already so prodigiously empty that there is nothing in it to clear away in the first place."

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:23.120
And it's this creative ability to think outside the box,

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:27.460
or without instructions that make master builders so powerful in the Lego-verse.

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:32.160
Except, maybe there's another quality than just pure creativity that matters.

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:35.670
You see, at the start of the movie, when Emmet is going to work at the construction site,

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:41.780
it seems like there's a lot of workers and infrastructure to accomplish that should be relatively simple tasks,

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:44.900
like connecting a 1x2 plate to another plate.

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:48.480
Hammers, cranes, hundreds of workers are involved in this process.

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:49.980
And yet later on in the movie,

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:53.000
we see the master builders doing things like this in seconds,

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:55.440
with pieces that are much larger than themselves.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:13:00.520
It really does take a feat of super strength to whip those LEGO creations together.

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:02.900
How much super strength? Glad you asked.

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:06.200
Because we're working with Legos, a widely used consumer product,

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:11.660
the data is available to us, and according to a report measuring strength required to build with Legos,

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:16.140
the brick separation on just 2 2x2 bricks measures out at 5.2 pounds.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:25.300
That is over a thousand times the force that we calculated for when Lego Batman kicked Alfred across the room.

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:29.600
That means that his strength to weight ratio suddenly skyrockets.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:41.780
It comes out to 791.48 that's the equivalent of our 200 pound Christian Bale exerting 158,295 pounds of force.

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:47.720
Just imagine some dude, or heck, Batman walking up to an adult male African bush elephant,

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:50.060
(the heaviest land animal on the planet)

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:51.880
and just, you know, lifting it up.

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:54.580
And then just casually lifting up 11 more.

00:13:54.580 --> 00:14:01.139
That is the equivalent of what Lego Batman is doing here every time he pries apart two Lego bricks.

00:14:01.139 --> 00:14:08.840
This means that not only does Lego Batman handily beat out our Nolan Batman, but he does so to an absurd degree.

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:13.860
Not only is the Lego Batman one of the best written and best performed Batmen in history,

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:17.600
but strangely enough, he's also the most super-powered.

